{id:"apollo11",world:"moon",name:"Apollo 11 Lunar Module Eagle",kind:"Descent stage",status:"Left on the Moon",year:"1969",glyph:"☾",image:"assets/images/nasa/as11-40-5927_orig.jpg",caption:"Apollo 11 astronaut Buzz Aldrin deploys the Early Apollo Scientific Experiments Package beside the Eagle descent stage · NASA/JSC · AS11-40-5927",summary:"The descent stage stayed behind after the ascent stage carried the astronauts back to orbit.",story:"The lower half of the Lunar Module acted as the landing platform. After the crew left the surface, the descent stage remained at Tranquility Base. It was not a failed spacecraft: it completed the job it was designed to do.",source:"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-059C"},
{id:"lrv",world:"moon",name:"Apollo Lunar Roving Vehicles",kind:"Crewed electric rover",status:"Parked on the Moon",year:"1971–72",glyph:"✧",image:"assets/images/nasa/as15-85-11471_orig.jpg",caption:"Apollo 15 commander David Scott seated in the Lunar Roving Vehicle; ALSEP equipment is also visible · NASA/JSC · AS15-85-11471",summary:"Three battery-powered rovers extended the astronauts' range on later Apollo missions.",story:"The Lunar Roving Vehicle let astronauts travel farther from their landing sites and carry more samples. The three flown rovers were left on the lunar surface when the crews departed.",source:"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1971-063C"},
{id:"alsep",world:"moon",name:"ALSEP scientific packages",kind:"Science instruments",status:"Switched off in 1977",year:"1969–72",glyph:"⌁",image:"assets/images/apollo14-alsep-as14-67-9361.jpg",caption:"Apollo 14 astronauts photographed the ALSEP deployment: the central station, experiment cables and equipment · NASA/JSC · AS14-67-9361",summary:"A network of instruments, including seismometers, studied the Moon after astronauts left.",story:"Apollo crews deployed scientific packages at several landing sites. Their instruments transmitted measurements back to Earth. The network was shut down on September 30, 1977, but the archived observations remain scientifically valuable.",source:"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1969-099C"},
{id:"surveyor3",world:"moon",name:"Surveyor 3",kind:"Robotic lander",status:"Partly retrieved · remains on the Moon",year:"1967",glyph:"◈",image:"assets/images/apollo12-surveyor3-as12-48-7134.jpg",caption:"Apollo 12 astronaut Pete Conrad examines Surveyor 3 before removing selected components for return to Earth · November 1969 · NASA (AS12-48-7134)",summary:"A robotic landing mission that helped prepare the way for Apollo.",story:"Surveyor 3 landed in 1967. Apollo 12 astronauts later visited the lander and brought selected components back to Earth for study, making it a rare meeting between robotic and crewed exploration.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/surveyor-3/"},
{id:"sojourner",world:"mars",name:"Sojourner",kind:"Mars rover",status:"Inactive on Mars",year:"1997",glyph:"✣",image:"assets/images/pathfinder-sojourner-second-rover-image-pia04315.jpg",caption:"Mars Pathfinder lander photographed by Sojourner; rover tracks cross the foreground · NASA/JPL/Caltech (PIA04315)",summary:"The first successful Mars rover explored near the Pathfinder lander.",story:"Sojourner demonstrated that a small mobile robot could explore another planet's surface. It operated during the Pathfinder mission in 1997 and remains at the landing site.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-pathfinder/"},
{id:"spirit",world:"mars",name:"Spirit",kind:"Mars Exploration Rover",status:"Inactive on Mars · Gusev Crater",year:"2004–10",glyph:"✣",image:"assets/images/spirit-landing-site-pia05117.webp",caption:"Spirit landing platform and surrounding airbags at Gusev Crater · Sol 16 · NASA/JPL-Caltech/Cornell (PIA05117)",summary:"A long-lived rover that explored Gusev Crater and travelled 7.7 kilometres.",story:"Spirit landed at Gusev Crater, a site chosen because it may once have held a lake inside a giant impact crater. This Sol 16 panorama shows the landing platform surrounded by crumpled airbags, with part of Spirit’s solar panels in the foreground. Spirit eventually travelled 7.7 kilometres (4.8 miles) before its mission ended.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-exploration-rovers-spirit-and-opportunity/"},
{id:"opportunity",world:"mars",name:"Opportunity",kind:"Mars Exploration Rover",status:"Inactive on Mars · Meridiani Planum",year:"2004–18",glyph:"✣",image:"assets/images/opportunity-lion-king-pia05755.webp",caption:"Opportunity’s Lion King panorama showing Eagle Crater and Meridiani Planum · sols 58–60 · NASA/JPL/Cornell (PIA05755)",summary:"A rover designed for 90 sols that explored for over 14 years and drove 45.16 kilometres.",story:"Opportunity landed at Meridiani Planum, where mineral deposits suggested a wet past. Its Lion King panorama, assembled from many images taken on sols 58 to 60, shows Eagle Crater and the surrounding plains. Opportunity travelled 45.16 kilometres (28.06 miles) before its final communication in June 2018.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-exploration-rovers-spirit-and-opportunity/"},
{id:"viking",world:"mars",name:"Viking 1 & 2 landers",kind:"Robotic landers",status:"Inactive on Mars",year:"1976",glyph:"⌂",image:"assets/images/nasa/PIA00381.jpg",caption:"Viking 1’s first surface photograph, including rocks, soil and part of a lander footpad · NASA/JPL · PIA00381",extraImage:"assets/images/nasa/PIA00396.jpg",extraCaption:"Viking 2’s first surface image at Utopia Planitia · NASA/JPL · PIA00396",galleryImages:[{src:"assets/images/viking-lander-artist-concept.jpg",caption:"Artist’s concept of a Viking lander on Mars · NASA/JPL"},{src:"assets/images/viking-lander-model.jpg",caption:"Model of the Viking landers · NASA/JPL"},{src:"assets/images/viking-spacecraft-artist-concept.jpg",caption:"Artist’s concept of the Viking orbiter and lander spacecraft · NASA/JPL"}],summary:"The first U.S. missions to operate successfully on the Martian surface.",story:"Viking 1 landed at Chryse Planitia on July 20, 1976; Viking 2 followed at Utopia Planitia on September 3. Each lander carried cameras, a robotic sampling arm, weather sensors and biology experiments. Their first images showed rocks, dust, the landers’ own footpads and wide horizons. Viking 1 sent its final transmission in 1982, while Viking 2 stopped communicating in 1980. Their orbiters also mapped much of Mars from above.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/viking-1/"},
{id:"insight",world:"mars",name:"InSight",kind:"Mars lander",status:"Mission ended · lander remains",year:"2018–22",glyph:"⌁",image:"assets/images/insight-seis-deployed-pia22956.jpg",caption:"InSight’s SEIS seismometer deployed on the Martian surface · December 19, 2018 · NASA/JPL-Caltech (PIA22956)",extraImage:"assets/images/insight-seis-deployment-pia22956.gif",extraCaption:"Animation of the SEIS deployment sequence · NASA/JPL-Caltech (PIA22956)",summary:"A stationary lander that listened for marsquakes and studied Mars' interior.",story:"InSight used a seismometer to detect marsquakes and learn about the planet's internal structure. NASA ended the mission after dust accumulation left the solar-powered lander unable to maintain power.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/insight/"},
{id:"phoenix",world:"mars",name:"Phoenix",kind:"Mars lander",status:"Inactive on Mars",year:"2008",glyph:"❋",image:"assets/images/phoenix-orbital-pia10701.jpg",caption:"Phoenix lander with its solar panels deployed, photographed from orbit by HiRISE · NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona · PIA10701",extraImage:"assets/images/phoenix-site-panorama-pia10735.jpg",extraCaption:"Phoenix’s own panorama shows the parachute, backshell, heat shield and landing impact area · NASA/JPL-Caltech/University of Arizona/Texas A&M · PIA10735",summary:"A polar lander that investigated water ice in Martian soil.",story:"Phoenix landed in the Martian arctic and used a robotic arm to dig into the soil. Its mission ended as seasonal darkness and cold arrived.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/phoenix/"},
{id:"ingenuity",world:"mars",name:"Ingenuity",kind:"Mars helicopter",status:"Stationary after rotor damage",year:"2021–24",glyph:"✥",image:"assets/images/ingenuity-deployed-pia24541.jpg",caption:"NASA’s Ingenuity helicopter on the Martian surface after deployment from Perseverance · NASA/JPL-Caltech (PIA24541)",summary:"The first aircraft to achieve powered, controlled flight on another planet.",story:"Ingenuity began as a technology demonstration and completed 72 flights. A hard landing during its final flight damaged rotor blades, ending flight operations; the helicopter remains on Mars.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/mars-2020-perseverance/ingenuity-mars-helicopter/"},
{id:"dawn",world:"beyond",name:"Dawn",kind:"Asteroid-belt orbiter",status:"Inert in orbit around Ceres",year:"2007–18",glyph:"◉",summary:"Explored Vesta and Ceres before running out of hydrazine.",story:"Dawn used ion propulsion to visit two large bodies in the asteroid belt. After its fuel was depleted, the spacecraft could no longer point its antenna or solar arrays reliably and its mission ended.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/"},
{id:"near",world:"beyond",name:"NEAR Shoemaker",kind:"Asteroid orbiter / lander",status:"Resting on Eros",year:"1996–2001",glyph:"◈",summary:"The first spacecraft to orbit an asteroid and then make a controlled touchdown.",story:"NEAR Shoemaker studied asteroid 433 Eros from orbit and completed a gentle touchdown in 2001. It was not designed as a lander, making the final contact an extraordinary bonus.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/near-shoemaker/"},
{id:"pioneer10",world:"beyond",name:"Pioneer 10",kind:"Deep-space probe",status:"Silent · still travelling",year:"1972–2003",glyph:"✦",summary:"The first spacecraft to pass through the asteroid belt and explore Jupiter up close.",story:"Pioneer 10 sent its last signal to Earth in 2003. It continues on an escape trajectory from the Solar System, though it no longer communicates with us.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/pioneer-10/"},
{id:"pioneer11",world:"beyond",name:"Pioneer 11",kind:"Deep-space probe",status:"No longer communicating · still travelling",year:"1973–1995",glyph:"✦",image:"assets/images/pioneer-plaque-spacecraft-arc.jpg",caption:"Pioneer plaque mounted within the spacecraft structure · NASA Ames Research Center archival photograph (ARC-1972-A72-2137)",extraImage:"assets/images/pioneer-plaque-closeup.png",extraCaption:"Close-up of the Pioneer plaque: a diagram intended to communicate information about humanity and the spacecraft’s origin · NASA/Ames",galleryImages:[{src:"assets/images/pioneer11-saturn-rings.jpeg",caption:"Saturn and its rings. Pioneer 11 passed within about 13,000 miles (21,000 km) of Saturn’s cloud tops on September 1, 1979 · NASA"},{src:"assets/images/pioneer-plaque-spacecraft-detail.jpg",caption:"Archival view of the plaque installed on the Pioneer spacecraft · NASA Ames Research Center (739463main_a73-9015-1)"}],summary:"The first spacecraft to fly past Saturn. Pioneer 11 also explored Jupiter and carried a message from Earth on a small metal plaque.",story:"Pioneer 11 launched on April 6, 1973. After travelling through the asteroid belt, it flew past Jupiter in December 1974 and used the planet’s gravity to head toward Saturn. On September 1, 1979, it became the first spacecraft to fly past Saturn, passing close to the planet and returning images and measurements of its rings, magnetic field and moons. It then continued outward, crossing Neptune’s orbital distance in 1990. NASA’s last contact was on September 30, 1995; a final short period of engineering data was received on November 24, 1995. Pioneer 11 is now silent and continues on a trajectory out of the Solar System.",source:"https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=1973-019A"},
{id:"kepler",world:"beyond",name:"Kepler Space Telescope",kind:"Space telescope",status:"Retired · drifting in orbit",year:"2009–18",glyph:"✧",summary:"Changed our understanding of how common planets are around other stars.",story:"Kepler detected thousands of planet candidates by watching for tiny dips in starlight. The mission ended after the spacecraft ran out of fuel in 2018.",source:"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/kepler/"}
,{id:"apollo12-catalogue",world:"moon",name:"Apollo 12 · surface objects",kind:"Lunar landing-site object catalogue",status:"Objects remain at Oceanus Procellarum; Surveyor 3 parts partly returned",year:"1969",glyph:"◉",image:"assets/images/apollo12-surveyor3-as12-48-7134.jpg",caption:"Apollo 12 astronaut Pete Conrad examines Surveyor 3 before collecting selected components · NASA · AS12-48-7134",summary:"A visual inventory of objects associated with Apollo 12, including the descent stage, central station and experiment equipment.",story:"Apollo 12 landed near the earlier Surveyor 3 spacecraft. The crew deployed a science station, collected lunar samples and visited Surveyor 3. Selected Surveyor components were brought back to Earth, while much of the mission hardware stayed on the Moon.",source:"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/final-catalogue-of-manmade-material-on-the-moon.pdf"},
{id:"apollo14-catalogue",world:"moon",name:"Apollo 14 · surface objects",kind:"Lunar landing-site object catalogue",status:"Hardware remains near Fra Mauro",year:"1971",glyph:"◉",image:"assets/images/apollo14-alsep-as14-67-9361.jpg",caption:"Apollo 14 astronauts photographed the ALSEP deployment: the central station, experiment cables and equipment · NASA/JSC · AS14-67-9361",summary:"The Apollo 14 site included ALSEP experiments, a central station, a lunar module and astronaut tools.",story:"Apollo 14 landed in the Fra Mauro highlands. Alan Shepard and Edgar Mitchell deployed ALSEP instruments, including seismic experiments designed to study vibrations travelling through the Moon. The crew also collected rocks and soil to help scientists understand the lunar highlands.",source:"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/final-catalogue-of-manmade-material-on-the-moon.pdf"},
{id:"apollo15-catalogue",world:"moon",name:"Apollo 15 · surface objects",kind:"Lunar landing-site object catalogue",status:"Hardware remains at Hadley–Apennine",year:"1971",glyph:"◉",image:"assets/images/nasa/as15-85-11471_orig.jpg",caption:"Apollo 15 commander David Scott seated in the Lunar Roving Vehicle; ALSEP equipment is also visible · NASA/JSC · AS15-85-11471",summary:"Apollo 15 left a lunar module, a rover, science equipment and many smaller tools at the Hadley–Apennine site.",story:"Apollo 15 was the first mission to use the Lunar Roving Vehicle. The rover allowed astronauts to travel farther from the landing site and bring back more samples. The crew also deployed an ALSEP station that continued returning science data after they left.",source:"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/final-catalogue-of-manmade-material-on-the-moon.pdf"},
{id:"apollo16-catalogue",world:"moon",name:"Apollo 16 · surface objects",kind:"Lunar landing-site object catalogue",status:"Hardware remains at Descartes Highlands",year:"1972",glyph:"◉",summary:"Apollo 16 left rover and scientific equipment, a central station and lunar module hardware.",story:"Apollo 16 landed in the Descartes Highlands on April 21, 1972. John Young and Charles Duke made three moonwalks totalling 20 hours and 14 minutes. They drove the rover about 27 km to visit several geology stops and collected 95.71 kg of rock and soil samples. Their deployed experiments helped scientists study the Moon’s surface and environment long after the astronauts returned home.",source:"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/final-catalogue-of-manmade-material-on-the-moon.pdf"},
{id:"apollo17-catalogue",world:"moon",name:"Apollo 17 · surface objects",kind:"Lunar landing-site object catalogue",status:"Hardware remains at Taurus–Littrow",year:"1972",glyph:"◉",summary:"Apollo 17 left a lunar rover, descent stage, ALSEP science equipment and many smaller objects.",story:"Apollo 17 was the final crewed Apollo landing. Gene Cernan and Harrison Schmitt explored the Taurus–Littrow valley using the rover. They deployed experiments, collected samples and left the descent stage and other equipment at the site.",source:"https://www.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/final-catalogue-of-manmade-material-on-the-moon.pdf"}

